{0 Support and known issues}

Behavior that differs from JavaScript Workers and native OCaml, and features
not supported yet.

{1 Differences from JavaScript Workers}

- Await only in the supported handler contexts. A function that JavaScript
  calls, such as a [setTimeout] callback, cannot await. See
  {{!page-async}Async and background work} for those contexts.
- {!Workero.URL} uses the WHATWG [URL], but {!Workero.URL.path} is decoded and
  only [http] and [https] URLs parse. See
  {{!page-url}URLs}.
- A response cannot have a status below 200 or above 599. The runtime rejects
  them, so {!Workero.Status} has [`Continue] and [`Switching_Protocols] only to
  read codes; use {!Workero.Websocket} for 101.

{1 Limits of streams, forms and WebSockets}

- Workero has no client-disconnect callback for a streamed response. A
  {!Workero.Stream.Writer.write} can reject when the client disconnects.
- {!Workero.Response.copy} and {!Workero.Response.of_stream} share the original
  body stream. It can be consumed only once. Call {!Workero.Response.clone}
  before reading if you need two copies.
- {!Workero.Request.form_data} holds the whole upload in memory. See
  {{!page-forms}Forms}.

{1 Binding limits}

- {{!page-queue}Queues}: consumers do not receive the content type.
  {!Workero.Queue.Message.body_json} on a [Text] body gives a JSON string.
  The other readers require compatible JavaScript value types and raise
  [Invalid_argument] for incompatible values. A [v8] body can be
  read only through {!Workero.Queue.Message.body_js}; workero producers cannot
  send [v8]. [send] and [send_batch] expose [delay_seconds], but no other send
  options. The runtime checks message size. Configure dead letter queues and
  [max_retries] in [wrangler.toml].
- {{!page-rate_limiter}Rate limiter}: the key is the only call argument. Set
  the limit and period in [wrangler.toml]. Counts are per location and
  approximate.
- {{!page-ai}Workers AI}: streaming answers and gateway options are not
  supported. {!Workero.Ai.run} raises [Invalid_argument] for a stream.
- {{!page-analytics_engine}Analytics Engine}: [write] returns no result. The
  runtime checks the limits of 20 blobs and 20 doubles. There is no query API.

{1 Durable Object limits}

- The storage API requires a SQLite-backed class. A class listed in
  [new_classes] can build, but its key-value and SQL calls fail at runtime.
  See {{!page-durable_objects}Durable Objects}.
- A stub supports [fetch] only. There are no RPC methods on a stub.
- Stored values are JSON. A [Date] that other code stores reads back as its ISO string and a [Map] as [{}]. A [BigInt] or a cyclic value raises [Invalid_argument].
- The function given to [register] cannot call {!Workero.Promise.await}. Load stored values in [~init]. Keep per-object state in closures.
- {!Workero.Durable_object.register} must run when the module starts. A [register] call that a condition skips is not exported.
- [workero build] needs [workerd]. The main module must be [index.js].
- A [block_concurrency_while] function that raises resets the object.
- SQL: [Int] values are exact only within ±[2^53]; a larger integer can
  read back as a [Float]. A whole-number [REAL] such as [2.0] reads back as
  [Int]. There is no asynchronous transaction. [Sql.run] returns only the
  number of rows written. {!Workero.Durable_object.Storage.transaction_sync}
  rejects {!Workero.Promise.await} with [Invalid_argument] and rolls back.
- {{!page-websockets}WebSockets}: a socket accepted with
  {!Workero.Websocket.accept} does not hibernate. Do not also call
  {!Workero.Durable_object.State.accept_websocket} on that socket. A class
  without [websocket_message] can accept hibernatable sockets, but does not
  handle their messages.
- The [workerd] tests cannot prove eviction and re-creation during hibernation,
  jurisdictions, or the tag and 2048-byte attachment limits. Test these in
  a deployed Worker.

{1 Not supported yet}

- The [tail] and [email] handlers.
- Workflows.
- RPC: [WorkerEntrypoint] and RPC methods on Durable Object stubs.
- Durable Objects without SQLite storage, and [State.container].
- Vectorize, Hyperdrive and the Send Email binding.
- [HTMLRewriter].
- Outbound TCP sockets ([connect]).
- [AbortController] and request cancellation.
- [crypto.subtle]. {!Workero.Crypto} has [random_uuid] and [random_bytes] only.
- Containers.

{1 Differences from native OCaml}

- [Sys.word_size] is 32, and [int] has 31 bits.
- There is no file system, except files that you embed. See
  {{!page-libraries}Supported libraries}.
- The program has no threads, sockets or sub-processes.

Successful compilation does not prove that a library's runtime dependencies
are available in a Worker. See {{!page-libraries}Supported libraries} and
{{!page-testing}Testing a worker}.

{1 Report a problem}

Open an issue in the
{{:https://github.com/ml-in-barcelona/workero}workero repository}. Include the
OCaml, wasm_of_ocaml and Wrangler versions, and the smallest worker that shows
the problem.
